WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:02.020
LARRY ARNN: The Fall
Convocation Talk

00:00:02.020 --> 00:00:05.450
is given by the
professor of the year.

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:09.720
This one is particularly
delightful to me.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:14.032
He will give the first speech
by a baby from this podium.

00:00:14.032 --> 00:00:20.640
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.130
Like half the people
in the county,

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:25.830
he is the offspring of
David and Janet Whalen.

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:31.040
So, you can't turn
around without running

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:32.848
into one of them.

00:00:32.848 --> 00:00:36.130
And this young man--

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:37.710
how old are you, Ben?

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:38.525
SPEAKER 2: 35.

00:00:38.525 --> 00:00:41.720
SPEAKER 1: 35, so I've
known him since he was 15.

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:42.700
See?

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:44.040
That was a subtraction problem.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:50.880
And, what was he
like when he was 15?

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:53.790
Well, in one way,
he is then exactly

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:58.260
as he will be when he's
80, he's baby faced.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:04.560
Also, he has that charming
smile and engaging way that

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:07.980
just makes him seem friendly.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:11.070
He grew up with a
bunch of friends.

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:15.330
Patrick did too, at least
two of the boys did.

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:19.050
And they're like
really rough customers.

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:23.370
They played rugby and they
hurt each other all the time,

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:26.130
and then they can
sing like angels.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:28.740
And so, of course, there's
larceny in the boy.

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:32.430
He and four of the
boys, three others,

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:37.170
went to Europe twice, and
bused all over Europe,

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:40.680
and came back with
money in their pocket.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.170
And I made him stand up in
my house years and years ago.

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:45.610
We'd been pheasant shooting,
and the boy can shoot too.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:50.700
We cooked the pheasants
and were eating them,

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:53.040
and I made those boys just
stop by and I made them sing,

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.260
and they really could sing.

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:58.230
And then, Ben Whalen
looks so dang pretty,

00:01:58.230 --> 00:02:00.837
you can see why people put
a lot of money in his bucket

00:02:00.837 --> 00:02:01.670
when he was singing.

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:09.759
He was the Whalen kid
that was most noticeable.

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:12.680
He's going to be a
college professor.

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:16.420
He just has such an open
warmth for knowing things,

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:20.650
and such a ready ability
to identify what is known,

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:21.850
what is best to be known.

00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:27.300
He took up English.

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:28.705
I guess you'd say
like his daddy.

00:02:32.243 --> 00:02:34.160
I remember he went to
the University of Dallas

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:36.260
and you went to
University of Nevada,

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:38.190
Las Vegas, where
oddly enough, there

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:40.011
was a great English professor.

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:46.840
I remember, it was a happy day
when David came into my office

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:51.010
and said, the English department
is interested in hiring

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:54.230
Ben for a one year appointment.

00:02:54.230 --> 00:02:58.132
I said that's the most wonderful
news, and he said wonderful?

00:02:58.132 --> 00:02:58.840
And I said, yeah.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:02.080
I said, you came to tell me that
you can't be involved in that.

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:05.405
And that means that
that boy belongs to me.

00:03:05.405 --> 00:03:07.030
And then sure enough
later, they wanted

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:10.060
to make him stick around here.

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:14.770
And then sure enough later, he
won the professor of the year.

00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:17.790
He is a golden young man.

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:19.870
Ben Whalen, professor
of the year.

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:38.490
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:40.130
BEN WHALEN: Thank you, Dr. Arnn.

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:42.960
That's a very kind introduction.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:44.520
I apologize to you all.

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:48.500
As I stepped on the
platform, my glasses broke.

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:52.400
It seems somehow fitting
in 2020 to give an address

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:53.160
to an audience.

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:55.928
I can't see, with a
text I can't read.

00:03:55.928 --> 00:03:59.760
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:04.950
On June 12th, 1592, when the
budding young actor Shakespeare

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:08.850
was just 28 years old and
fresh on the scene of London,

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:11.880
a riot rocked the
English capital.

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:14.580
Apparently, an apprentice
to a felt maker

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:17.310
had been arrested
and thrown in prison

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:20.010
under dubious circumstances.

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:22.320
His fellow felt
making apprentices

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:25.500
gathered in the Southern
District to protest.

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:28.560
It was an area pocketed
with taverns and theaters,

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:31.140
and was where
Shakespeare performed.

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:35.010
The protest drew a large
crowd and became violent.

00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:36.660
Supported, as the
mayor of London

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:39.690
describes things, by
quote, "a great number

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:45.840
of loose and masterless men,
act for such purposes as riot."

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:49.140
The mayor, William Webb,
responded with vigor,

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:52.800
arresting the principal and
violent actors of the riot.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:54.870
The Elizabethan government
was made uneasy,

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:58.350
however, and when the rumors
spread that the film maker

00:04:58.350 --> 00:05:01.380
apprentices were
planning a second tumult,

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:04.170
the Privy Council
deployed an armed watch,

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:06.630
forbade certain forms
of public gathering,

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:10.080
and shut down the
theaters for three months.

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:11.820
It was a hard blow
for those who had made

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:13.910
the theater their profession.

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:15.870
Indeed, the suspension
of the theaters

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:20.310
lasted far longer than the
initially planned three months.

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:23.790
Later in the year, a large
outbreak of the bubonic plague

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:25.710
swept through England.

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:28.500
And as the government tried to
limit the spread of the plague,

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:30.750
it forced the theaters
to remain closed

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:33.600
for much of the next
year and a half.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:35.880
What was Shakespeare to do?

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:38.940
Here was a young man
just come to London,

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:43.020
just beginning his career,
facing a country convulsed

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:46.740
by riots and a deadly disease.

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:48.600
Shakespeare did
what any of us would

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:50.380
have done in such a situation.

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:52.430
He organized a Zoom
rendition of Hamlet.

00:05:52.430 --> 00:05:54.890
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:54.890 --> 00:05:57.830
Notably, with his means
of employment gone,

00:05:57.830 --> 00:06:00.410
Shakespeare turns to
his poetry, producing

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:03.500
the only narrative poems
that he ever wrote,

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:06.380
Venus and Adonis and
The Rape of Lucrece.

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:09.440
Both of these are some of the
very finest narrative poems

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:11.390
in our literary tradition.

00:06:11.390 --> 00:06:13.490
Venus and Adonis, a
playful meditation

00:06:13.490 --> 00:06:16.430
on love and desire,
while The Rape of Lucrece

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:20.090
is a serious examination
of the nature of suffering,

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:22.370
the abuse of power,
the foundations

00:06:22.370 --> 00:06:26.060
of human government, and ancient
Rome's transition from monarchy

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:28.010
to a Republic.

00:06:28.010 --> 00:06:30.020
We probably would
be willing enough

00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:33.050
to excuse Shakespeare
had the plague,

00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:37.580
with its many deadly terrors
and surely daily nuisances

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:41.000
and worries, occupied
all of his attention

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:45.020
and prevented him from writing,
or at least drive him well.

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:47.030
We probably would
be willing enough

00:06:47.030 --> 00:06:51.050
to excuse Shakespeare had
his imagination been occupied

00:06:51.050 --> 00:06:54.950
by visions of hordes of felt
making apprentices pouring down

00:06:54.950 --> 00:06:57.980
the streets, felt in
hand, ready to light it,

00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:00.980
and watched London burn.

00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:04.280
Indeed, it is rather
remarkable that although waves

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:07.640
of the bubonic plague swept
through England several times

00:07:07.640 --> 00:07:10.160
during Shakespeare's
youth and adulthood,

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:13.880
for instance, the
year he was born,

00:07:13.880 --> 00:07:17.000
it killed one out of every
five people in his hometown

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:19.940
of Stratford, one out of five.

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:23.650
Shakespeare rarely
mentions it in his works.

00:07:23.650 --> 00:07:27.190
It is surprising, even
perhaps a little odd,

00:07:27.190 --> 00:07:30.610
to find how little notice,
how little attention,

00:07:30.610 --> 00:07:34.870
Shakespeare seems to give
to this deadly disease.

00:07:34.870 --> 00:07:36.850
It's almost as if
Shakespeare is calling

00:07:36.850 --> 00:07:40.510
us to look at other
things, higher things

00:07:40.510 --> 00:07:44.340
than the banal facts
of disease and death.

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:47.320
And it's not that Shakespeare
is an escapist in his poetry,

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:49.630
nor is he a blinded idealist.

00:07:49.630 --> 00:07:53.290
As I mentioned, he wrote
about suffering extensively.

00:07:53.290 --> 00:07:56.040
But Shakespeare is
less interested in all

00:07:56.040 --> 00:07:59.410
of the different things that
might cause mankind misery

00:07:59.410 --> 00:08:04.030
or death, and is more interested
in how we might suffer or die

00:08:04.030 --> 00:08:05.320
well.

00:08:05.320 --> 00:08:08.740
How we might find meaning
or redemption, or grace,

00:08:08.740 --> 00:08:13.210
in the concrete fact that
we all will suffer, and are

00:08:13.210 --> 00:08:15.610
bound to become dust.

00:08:15.610 --> 00:08:17.860
Faced with that grim
reality, Shakespeare

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:20.470
turns to the higher
things, to search out

00:08:20.470 --> 00:08:24.490
the springs of mercy and
justice, of love and nobility,

00:08:24.490 --> 00:08:27.560
of sin and forgiveness.

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:30.490
But if Shakespeare,
confronted with civil disorder

00:08:30.490 --> 00:08:34.539
and terrible disease, was still
able to turn to higher things,

00:08:34.539 --> 00:08:36.039
perhaps that was
simply because he

00:08:36.039 --> 00:08:39.700
was Shakespeare, a genius
unequaled in our tradition.

00:08:39.700 --> 00:08:41.710
Perhaps in his
greatness, we can't

00:08:41.710 --> 00:08:44.710
find an example for ourselves.

00:08:44.710 --> 00:08:47.320
Let me tell a second story that
I take from James Shapiro's

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:50.290
book, Shakespeare in America.

00:08:50.290 --> 00:08:54.190
In January 1846,
part of the US army

00:08:54.190 --> 00:08:57.460
found itself stationed
in Corpus Christi, Texas

00:08:57.460 --> 00:09:01.090
to prepare for the outbreak
of the Mexican-American war.

00:09:01.090 --> 00:09:05.020
Corpus Christi was described
by one officer as quote,

00:09:05.020 --> 00:09:08.850
"the most murderous,
thieving, gambling, cutthroat,

00:09:08.850 --> 00:09:11.850
godforsaken, hole in Texas."

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:15.320
And if you know Texas,
that's saying a lot.

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:19.100
The troops were growing idle,
and idleness and godforsaken

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:21.740
holes are not a good match.

00:09:21.740 --> 00:09:26.090
To counteract this John B.
Magruder had the soldiers build

00:09:26.090 --> 00:09:28.670
a large theater, and
then organized them

00:09:28.670 --> 00:09:31.880
for the production of several
plays, Shakespeare's Othello

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:33.590
among them.

00:09:33.590 --> 00:09:37.350
In the initial casting,
the part of Desdemona,

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:39.800
Othello's beautiful
wife, was given

00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:43.820
to James Longstreet, the man
whom Robert E. Lee would soon

00:09:43.820 --> 00:09:46.530
call his old warhorse.

00:09:46.530 --> 00:09:48.820
Longstreet was a
big man, standing

00:09:48.820 --> 00:09:54.770
at 6 foot 2, more than 200
pounds, and with a long beard.

00:09:54.770 --> 00:09:58.610
They decided he didn't look
quite right for Desdemona,

00:09:58.610 --> 00:10:01.880
and instead, they gave the
part to the shorter, lighter,

00:10:01.880 --> 00:10:04.940
Ulysses S. Grant.

00:10:04.940 --> 00:10:09.440
Grant, however, was too brisk,
and the soldier playing Othello

00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:12.260
grew frustrated,
complaining quote,

00:10:12.260 --> 00:10:15.470
"I cannot pump up any
sentiment with Grant dressed up

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:16.682
as Desdemona."

00:10:19.460 --> 00:10:22.670
They eventually sent to have
a professional actress come

00:10:22.670 --> 00:10:26.330
and so resolved the difficulty,
though Longstreet did note

00:10:26.330 --> 00:10:29.540
in his memoirs that
Grant quote, "looked

00:10:29.540 --> 00:10:32.540
very like a girl dressed
up but he did not

00:10:32.540 --> 00:10:35.300
have much sentiment."

00:10:35.300 --> 00:10:38.750
Now, I admit that I
share this story in part,

00:10:38.750 --> 00:10:41.390
because I simply delight in
the thought of Ulysses S.

00:10:41.390 --> 00:10:44.450
Grant, future president
of the United States

00:10:44.450 --> 00:10:49.370
in a dress playing the
gentle, elegant Desdemona.

00:10:49.370 --> 00:10:51.950
But the more I thought
about it, the more

00:10:51.950 --> 00:10:55.250
I've understood the
significance of this action.

00:10:55.250 --> 00:11:01.940
Magruder, Longstreet, Grant,
all tough men, men of action,

00:11:01.940 --> 00:11:05.000
men preparing for
bloody deeds, men

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:08.180
who would play decisive roles
in the terrible conflict that

00:11:08.180 --> 00:11:11.870
would determine the course of
our great nation's history.

00:11:11.870 --> 00:11:14.690
When caught in a
godforsaken hole trying

00:11:14.690 --> 00:11:17.480
to manage soldiers
and prepare for a war,

00:11:17.480 --> 00:11:20.530
they too turned to
the higher things,

00:11:20.530 --> 00:11:26.270
to the fine and delicate
domestic tragedy of Othello.

00:11:26.270 --> 00:11:29.425
This wasn't just busy
work for idle hands.

00:11:29.425 --> 00:11:30.800
They could have
made the soldiers

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:35.420
dig ditches easily enough, or
any other form of manual labor.

00:11:35.420 --> 00:11:38.150
No, Magruder,
Longstreet, and Grant

00:11:38.150 --> 00:11:40.430
were engaged in a
much harder toil,

00:11:40.430 --> 00:11:44.390
of civilizing men,
of building culture,

00:11:44.390 --> 00:11:47.900
of taking the finest things
that have been handed down to us

00:11:47.900 --> 00:11:52.790
and making them live
again, even in Texas.

00:11:52.790 --> 00:11:56.130
But if Shakespeare's London
or Grant's Corpus Christi

00:11:56.130 --> 00:11:59.180
seemed too far away from
us, we are at Hillsdale

00:11:59.180 --> 00:12:01.910
blessed to have a
long and noble history

00:12:01.910 --> 00:12:04.290
that we might look to as well.

00:12:04.290 --> 00:12:07.220
We all know, of course our
college's great triumph

00:12:07.220 --> 00:12:09.890
during the American Civil
War, when our young men went

00:12:09.890 --> 00:12:12.830
to fight for our Union and
when our college nevertheless

00:12:12.830 --> 00:12:15.500
continued its mission to
furnish all persons who

00:12:15.500 --> 00:12:18.470
wish irrespective
of nation, color,

00:12:18.470 --> 00:12:23.960
or sex, a literary, scientific,
and theological education.

00:12:23.960 --> 00:12:26.390
These are the higher
things, and our college

00:12:26.390 --> 00:12:30.620
followed its mission even in
the darkest days of our country.

00:12:30.620 --> 00:12:33.680
But given our current
situation, my thoughts

00:12:33.680 --> 00:12:37.790
turn to the Hillsdale
College of 1918.

00:12:37.790 --> 00:12:41.690
In the spring of that year,
with World War I raging,

00:12:41.690 --> 00:12:44.420
the United States government
established the Student Army

00:12:44.420 --> 00:12:48.680
Training Corps, a program where
male students at liberal arts

00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:52.610
colleges across the country were
enlisted in a program jointly

00:12:52.610 --> 00:12:56.510
administered by the
government and the colleges,

00:12:56.510 --> 00:12:59.660
in which the young men
received both military training

00:12:59.660 --> 00:13:02.180
and a liberal arts education.

00:13:02.180 --> 00:13:05.570
The men were formally enlisted
as privates in the US army

00:13:05.570 --> 00:13:08.090
and subject to military law.

00:13:08.090 --> 00:13:11.240
Additionally, the US government
paid their tuition and fees

00:13:11.240 --> 00:13:15.110
and they received a
salary of $30 a month.

00:13:15.110 --> 00:13:19.280
Hillsdale College was selected
to host one such program,

00:13:19.280 --> 00:13:23.600
and 195 of our young
men enlisted in it.

00:13:23.600 --> 00:13:26.930
There was a barracks built right
there, down Manning Street,

00:13:26.930 --> 00:13:29.390
in the old shoe factory.

00:13:29.390 --> 00:13:32.300
The young men of Hillsdale
lived in this barracks, engaged

00:13:32.300 --> 00:13:34.730
in drills, parades,
and training,

00:13:34.730 --> 00:13:37.790
while also attending
college classes.

00:13:37.790 --> 00:13:41.900
We have wonderful records in
The Collegian of the goings on.

00:13:41.900 --> 00:13:43.975
Of course, with the
men busy in training,

00:13:43.975 --> 00:13:46.100
The Collegian was mostly
written by the young women

00:13:46.100 --> 00:13:47.450
of our college.

00:13:47.450 --> 00:13:50.510
And we have many little notes
about how fine the men looked

00:13:50.510 --> 00:13:54.530
in their uniforms, and how
lonely the dining hall was

00:13:54.530 --> 00:13:56.510
with all of the men gone.

00:13:56.510 --> 00:13:58.010
In the evenings,
to Hillsdale women

00:13:58.010 --> 00:14:00.590
would gather down the street
to watch the Hillsdale men

00:14:00.590 --> 00:14:04.880
parade for the lowering of the
flag and the retreat ceremony,

00:14:04.880 --> 00:14:06.650
and then return to
their residences

00:14:06.650 --> 00:14:09.450
and do what they have always
done at Hillsdale College,

00:14:09.450 --> 00:14:11.990
surpass the young men in their
study of Latin and Greek.

00:14:11.990 --> 00:14:14.840
[LAUGHTER]

00:14:14.840 --> 00:14:18.470
But in 1918, we didn't
only have a world rending

00:14:18.470 --> 00:14:21.740
conflict of mind
boggling proportions,

00:14:21.740 --> 00:14:23.600
we also saw the
terrible outbreak

00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:26.690
of the infamous
Spanish influenza.

00:14:26.690 --> 00:14:31.680
Hillsdale, of course, was not
untouched by the 1918 pandemic.

00:14:31.680 --> 00:14:33.620
Students became sick.

00:14:33.620 --> 00:14:35.960
And one of our
students, a young man

00:14:35.960 --> 00:14:39.110
in the temporary barracks of
the Student Army Training Corps,

00:14:39.110 --> 00:14:42.740
died of the virus in
December of that year.

00:14:42.740 --> 00:14:48.350
He was Harry Kelly, part
of the class of '22, 1922.

00:14:48.350 --> 00:14:51.170
He was 21 years old,
and The Collegian

00:14:51.170 --> 00:14:55.760
reports that he quote, "rendered
faithful and efficient service

00:14:55.760 --> 00:14:58.700
in the Student Army
Training Corps."

00:14:58.700 --> 00:15:01.510
I want to think about
him for a moment.

00:15:01.510 --> 00:15:05.890
He walked the same
campus where we now sit.

00:15:05.890 --> 00:15:08.800
He saw the same hills.

00:15:08.800 --> 00:15:11.350
I have trees in my
backyard that are

00:15:11.350 --> 00:15:16.270
much older than he was, and he
might have seen or sat under it

00:15:16.270 --> 00:15:18.580
had he strolled past the
Hungry Howie's of yore

00:15:18.580 --> 00:15:21.430
onto Norwood Avenue.

00:15:21.430 --> 00:15:23.960
Why was he here?

00:15:23.960 --> 00:15:25.780
What was he doing?

00:15:25.780 --> 00:15:30.730
Why, with the world falling
apart in disease and war,

00:15:30.730 --> 00:15:33.250
did the young man come
to Hillsdale College

00:15:33.250 --> 00:15:35.710
to enlist in the Student
Army Training Corps

00:15:35.710 --> 00:15:39.490
and study both soldiery
and the liberal arts?

00:15:39.490 --> 00:15:44.930
Why did he do this, risking his
very life which he duly paid?

00:15:44.930 --> 00:15:46.660
Why did the young
women of the college

00:15:46.660 --> 00:15:51.460
attend, despite the
conflict and the influenza?

00:15:51.460 --> 00:15:54.230
Why study Latin, or
Shakespeare, or physics,

00:15:54.230 --> 00:15:56.530
at such a time when
there are so many more

00:15:56.530 --> 00:16:00.260
pressing and practical concerns?

00:16:00.260 --> 00:16:04.250
Our studies here
point beyond and above

00:16:04.250 --> 00:16:07.610
the quotidian mess of
conflict and disease

00:16:07.610 --> 00:16:11.310
that is the constant
companion of mankind.

00:16:11.310 --> 00:16:14.480
It isn't that we ignore
or dismiss the suffering

00:16:14.480 --> 00:16:16.970
that conflict and
disease brings.

00:16:16.970 --> 00:16:19.280
These are real aspects
of our existence,

00:16:19.280 --> 00:16:21.560
and we must exercise
prudence and fortitude

00:16:21.560 --> 00:16:24.020
in confronting them,
as well I think,

00:16:24.020 --> 00:16:27.620
as praying for the gifts of
faith, hope, and charity.

00:16:27.620 --> 00:16:31.280
But we must never be
governed wholly by them.

00:16:31.280 --> 00:16:34.130
To have one's actions,
finally determined

00:16:34.130 --> 00:16:38.090
by conflict or disease
is to be subject to them,

00:16:38.090 --> 00:16:40.300
to become slave to them.

00:16:40.300 --> 00:16:42.650
The free man or
woman, deliberately

00:16:42.650 --> 00:16:46.970
educated man or
woman, retains always

00:16:46.970 --> 00:16:50.330
the sense of that which
is higher and finer.

00:16:50.330 --> 00:16:54.260
Those permanent things,
those transcendent truths,

00:16:54.260 --> 00:16:56.510
that rightly order
our existence,

00:16:56.510 --> 00:17:01.760
and give our lives meaning
even in the face of suffering,

00:17:01.760 --> 00:17:04.450
And so, friends, I can only
end in expressing my thanks

00:17:04.450 --> 00:17:06.339
and admiration.

00:17:06.339 --> 00:17:09.970
I am deeply grateful
to our administration,

00:17:09.970 --> 00:17:13.960
to our students, and to
the faculty and staff.

00:17:13.960 --> 00:17:17.050
Despite the tremendous headwinds
we have faced as a college,

00:17:17.050 --> 00:17:20.560
we continue to pursue our
mission, and the great and good

00:17:20.560 --> 00:17:22.210
of the noble endeavor
we are engaged

00:17:22.210 --> 00:17:25.180
in with vigor and courage.

00:17:25.180 --> 00:17:29.200
I have never been so proud to
be a part of Hillsdale College

00:17:29.200 --> 00:17:32.380
as in this year, in
these trying times.

00:17:32.380 --> 00:17:33.220
Thank you.

00:17:33.220 --> 00:17:35.070
[APPLAUSE]

